    "https://careers.usc.edu.au/jobs/lecturer-in-first-nations-contemporary-art-and-design-identified-position-unisc-sunshine-coast-qld-australia": {
      "title": "Lecturer in First Nations Contemporary Art and Design (Identified position)",
      "description": "Lecturer in First Nations Contemporary Art and Design (Identified position) - UniSC Sunshine Coast, QLD, Australia Contact Us Candidate login Explore Opportunities Academic jobs Professional jobs Casual jobs Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander jobs Recruitment process Employer information Applicant Settings Kiosk mode < BACK TO EXPLORE OPPORTUNITIES Lecturer in First Nations Contemporary Art and Design (Identified position) UniSC Sunshine Coast, QLD, Australia, 4556 Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Academic LB $118,481 - $140,567 +17% super School of Business and Creative Industries Show More Show Less Full-Time Add to favourites View favourites School of Business and Creative Industries | UniSC Sunshine Coast in Sippy Downs located on the traditional lands of the Kabi Kabi/Gubbi Gubbi Peoples Academic Level B - $118,481 - $140,567 p.a., plus up to 17% employer superannuation Full-time, ongoing appointment The School of Business and Creative Industries (SBCI) School of Business and Creative Industries: Ready to make an impact in a dynamic world? Are you driven to make a difference in a constantly evolving world? We invite passionate educators and industry experts to join our inspiring team within the School of Business and Creative Industries (SBCI). Our mission is “educating to make a difference”, empowering students with innovative and sustainable practices to tackle global challenges head-on. With strong industry partnerships and a focus on practical experience, you'll play a key role in preparing future leaders and practitioners for exciting careers. The Opportunity The School of Business and Creative Industries is seeking a Lecturer in First Nations Contemporary Art and Design to contribute to the strategic development of Design at UniSC. This is a distinctive opportunity for an active practising First Nations contemporary artist and/or designer to shape culturally responsive design education, creative practice research and community engagement. You will play a key role in strengthening the Bachelor of Design by embedding First Nations knowledges, perspectives and protocols across teaching and learning, supporting students to develop ethical, inclusive, socially responsible and sustainable approaches to contemporary design practice. A key focus of the role will be teaching in Design on Country, an immersive learning experience exploring what it means to work respectfully on Kabi Kabi, Jinibara and Butchulla Country. Through fieldwork, cultural experiences and Indigenous-led perspectives, you will support students to engage meaningfully with Country, culture and contemporary design practice. Working reciprocally with Traditional Custodians, artists, designers, cultural knowledge holders and community organisations, you will bring cultural authority and contemporary creative practice into the classroom, while contributing to creative practice and interdisciplinary research and supporting the next generation of First Nations artists, designers and researchers. For more information please visit us online: School of Business and Creative Industries You will: Shape First Nations design education: embed First Nations knowledges, perspectives and cultural protocols across the Bachelor of Design and contribute to innovative, culturally responsive curriculum. Teach and create on Country: lead and contribute to Design on Country , connecting students with Kabi Kabi, Jinibara and Butchulla Country through place-based, fieldwork and immersive learning. Grow creative practice and community impact: develop creative practice research and outputs while building reciprocal relationships with Traditional Custodians, artists, designers, cultural knowledge holders and communities. A bout you: You will be an active practising First Nations contemporary artist and/or designer with demonstrated cultural connections to Kabi Kabi, Jinibara or Butchulla Country. You will bring contemporary creative practice, cultural knowledge and a strong understanding of First Nations ways of knowing, being and doing, together with the ability to contribute to teaching, research and engagement at university level. You will be a collaborative and culturally capable educator who can build respectful and reciprocal relationships with Traditional Custodians, artists, designers, cultural knowledge holders and communities. You will be committed to creating culturally safe learning environments and supporting students to engage ethically and meaningfully with Country, culture and contemporary creative practice. You will possess: A doctoral qualification in First Nations contemporary art, design or a related creative discipline, or equivalent standing through significant professional and creative practice, cultural knowledge, community engagement and scholarly achievement. A strong record as an active practising contemporary artist and/or designer, with creative outcomes recognised by communities, the arts and design sector or relevant professional networks. Experience, or demonstrated capacity, to design and deliver effective, culturally safe and inclusive learning experiences in higher education, professional, community or cultural contexts. Demonstrated knowledge and application of First Nations ways of knowing, being and doing, cultural protocols and Indigenous Cultural and Intellectual Property, with an ethical and reciprocal approach to working with Country and community. A developing record of scholarly and creative practice outputs appropriate to Academic Level B, including exhibitions, creative works, practice-led research, publications, community-led outcomes or other recognised research outputs. The ability to establish and sustain respectful relationships with Traditional Custodians, artists, designers, cultural knowledge holders, communities and professional partners, and contribute positively to a collegial academic environment. Identified position and support The University of the Sunshine Coast considers that being Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander is a genuine occupational requirement for this position under s25 of the Anti-Discrimination Act 1991 (Qld) and the filling of this position constitutes a special/equal opportunity measure under section 8(1) of the Racial Discrimination Act 1975 (Cth). The position is therefore only open to Australian Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander people. We recognise that cultural knowledge, community leadership, lived experience and professional expertise are valuable strengths and encourage applicants from diverse career pathways to apply. How to apply Position description: 494892_Lecturer in First Nations Contemporary Art and Design.pdf Applications close 11:55pm, Sunday 1 November 2026. a resume, and a response to selection criteria. Noting that within the application form, we will request a statement addressing each of the selection criteria contained within the position description. Have questions or want to know more? For a confidential discussion, please contact: Dr Leah Barclay Discipline Lead and Senior Lecturer in Design - School of Business and Creative Industries Email: lbarclay@usc.edu.au Further information regarding how to prepare your application is available on our website: Join our team. Discover UniSC Our Culture & Benefits: At UniSC, our culture is built around flexibility, respect and trust, with employment conditions that recognise cultural responsibilities alongside professional commitments. You will have access to: Indigenous‑specific employment provisions under UniSC’s Enterprise Agreement, including paid cultural leave and support for cultural and community responsibilities; flexible work arrangements that support connection to Country, community and family; culturally informed wellbeing support, including access to employee assistance services; professional development and career progression opportunities, with support that recognises achievement relative to opportunity; respect for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander intellectual and cultural knowledges, with clear protections around ownership, consent and ethical use. modern teaching, research and work environments across our Sunshine Coast and South East Queensland campuses; and a collegial, values‑led workplace where relationships, respect and care for people matter. Support doesn't end at recruitment. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander employees are supported through culturally informed onboarding, professional development, mentoring opportunities and ongoing cultural support throughout their employment journey. The successful candidate will join a growing network of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander academic and professional staff across UniSC and be supported through established Indigenous leadership, employment and community engagement initiatives. These benefits sit alongside UniSC’s broader commitment to providing a safe, supportive and flexible workplace where Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff can build sustainable, meaningful careers. For more information, visit https://careers.usc.edu.au/employer-information < BACK TO EXPLORE OPPORTUNTIES About UniSC For more information about UniSC's employment benefits and culture, including employee blogs, visit our employer information page . Recommended for you Clinical Trials Associate - Pharmacy Health Hub Morayfield Clinical Trials, Individual contributor, L5 $82,843 - $93,358 +17% super Join UniSC Clinical Trials at Health Hub Morayfield and support the safe, accurate and compliant management of investigational products used in clinical research. Health, Safety and Wellbeing Business Partner UniSC Sunshine Coast L8 $118,986 - $134,756 +17% super, People and Culture, Professional Make a meaningful impact at UniSC by strengthening workplace safety, supporting our people to thrive and helping shape a culture of wellbeing, respect and inclusion. Associate Professor in Urgent Care UniSC Sunshine Coast Academic, LD $174,423 - $192,092 +17% super, School of Health Lead the future of urgent care education and research while shaping practical learning, supporting academic capability and building strong connections with the health sector. Casual Academics (Nursing) Sunshine Coast Health Institute. UniSC Caboolture. UniSC Fraser Coast. UniSC Gympie. UniSC Sunshine Coast. UniSC Thompson Institute. UniSC Moreton Bay Academic, Casual Academic, Nursing, School of Health Join UniSC’s School of Health in 2027 as a Casual Academic (Nursing) and share your contemporary nursing expertise and professional experience with the next generation of nursing professionals. LEGAL Privacy Copyright Disclaimer Right to Information Data Collection Preferences The University of the Sunshine Coast acknowledges the Traditional Custodians of the land on which we live, work and study. We pay our respects to local Indigenous Elders past, present and emerging and recognise the strength, resilience and capacity of all Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people. ABN 28 441 859 157 | CRICOS Provider No. 01595D | TEQSA Provider No. PRV12082 Candidate Details This website uses cookies. We use cookies to personalise content such as job recommendations, and to analyse our traffic. You consent to our cookies if you click \"I Accept\". If you click on \"I Do Not Accept\", then we will not use cookies but you may have a deteriorated user experience. You can change your settings by clicking on the Settings link on the top right of the device. By continuing, you agree that you have read our Privacy Statement I do not accept I accept"
